window.RSR_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "address", "title": "Address Procedures", "category": "Treasurer · Accounting Procedures", "description": "Confidence levels and procedures for documenting, validating, and updating owner and parcel mailing addresses in QuickBooks.", "pdf": "./documents/address-procedures.pdf", "odt": "./source/Address_Procedures.odt", "content": "Address Procedures\nTREASURER\nCONFIDENCE LEVEL\nDetermine confidence level of the new address information according to the source of the data. Insert data into the record via the procedures set up for that confidence level.\nConfidence Levels:\nHigh Confidence:\nDirect email request from the client.\nDirect postal mail request from the client.\nPhone call from the client.\nText message from the client.\nNote from client included with payment.\nPOA initiated contact with client via phone, email, or text msg.\nMedium Confidence:\nEscrow papers received during property transfers.\nAddress listed with Square payment processing.\nUSPS address service notices.\nUSPS mail returned notices containing new address.\nAddress listed on latest county tax payment.\nCounty parcel records.\nWord of mouth from a source other than the client.\nLow Confidence:\nAddress on check.\nPhone book.\nInternet search.\nOther.\nProcedures: Using the AirGapped Accounting Computer access QuickBooks link on desktop. DO NOT CONNECT THIS MACHINE TO THE INTERNET\nHigh Confidence Level Address:\nSelect Owner’s account to edit on active customer level (see Customer Information header)\nRefer to Changing an Address section below\nCreate time stamped note only in customer record.\nChange address in customer record.\nChange address in each parcel record.\nMedium Confidence:\nUse escrow data to set up new customer in database. This address may no longer be good if customer is moving. Sometimes realtor or escrow agent will have an updated address.\nCreate time stamped note only in customer record.\nChange address in customer record.\nChange address in each parcel record.\nLow Confidence:\nCreate timestamped note only in customer record.\nDo not change address in customer or parcel records.\nIf mail is returned, try new address to verify validity, edit note to indicate attempt to use new address.\nIf all efforts repeatedly fail – mark address in Customer Information with “INVALID ADDRESS”.\nChanging an Address to Customer Information\nSelect Owner’s account to edit on active customer level (see Customer Information header)\nEnter Notes tab\nManage Notes: Add New.\nClick on Date/Time Stamp, this inserts time stamp into note\nType your initials after the time stamp\nType source and mechanism new address data was received from REFER to CONFIDENCE LEVEL eg: text message or mail from owner\nType “Old address”\nType “New address” and enter new address\nClose NOTE by pressing OK (must exit note to locate old address)\nLocate Old Address:\nClick EDIT next to Customer Information\nSelect address and copy, then press CANCEL\nDouble click previously created Note\nPaste old address beneath Old Address section\nSelect and COPY previously typed New Address in the Note\nPress OK to commit changes, this creates an audit trail for the address change.\nEnter New Address to Customer Record\nClick EDIT next to Customer Information\nSelect address and PASTE\nPress OK to commit changes\nChanging an Address to Parcel Record\nSelect first Parcel Record to edit on Job Information level (see Job Information header to confirm correct location)\nClick EDIT next to Job Information\nSelect address and PASTE (The previously copied address should still be in your clipboard)\nPress OK to commit changes\nRepeat same procedure for each parcel owned by Customer\nGenerally, notes are not added to Parcel Records UNLESS it is something specific to that one parcel.\nContact information is NOT entered in Parcel Records except for the postal address. Prevents duplicate information when parcels are sold."}, {"id": "secretary-checks", "title": "Check Payment Processing — Secretary", "category": "Secretary · Accounting Procedures", "description": "Procedures for receiving payments, organizing separate deposits, preparing deposit packets, making bank deposits, and transferring packets to the Treasurer.", "pdf": "./documents/check-processing-secretary.pdf", "odt": "./source/Check_Payment_Processing_Secretary.odt", "content": "Check Payment Processing\nSECRETARY\nCollect mail from PO Box 2279 and any hand delivered payments1. Hand delivered payments can only be turned over at an official RSR function or meeting and must be handed to no less than two board members.\n. Hand delivered payments can only be turned over at an official RSR function or meeting and must be handed to no less than two board members.\nOpen mail, discard junk mail, sort any payments\nWhen money is received, determine whether it’s an Owner Payment, an Escrow Payment, a Mortgage Company payment or Equipment Sales or Purchases2. There can be instances where there may be a partial refund, a refund of overpayment, or a return of payment for partial quantities, double payments, or canceled orders.\n. There can be instances where there may be a partial refund, a refund of overpayment, or a return of payment for partial quantities, double payments, or canceled orders.\nOwner Payments (General Assessments)\nPrepare a bank deposit for that group of checks\nUsing duplicate deposit slips – list last name and amount per block\nAdd sum total of checks and compare to sum total on deposit slip\nCompare return address of envelope with address on check3. Refer to Address Procedures for processing address changes.\n. Refer to Address Procedures for processing address changes.\nIf same discard envelope\nIf different include envelope in Deposit Packet\nIf notes are included with payment include in Deposit Packet\nEscrow Payment (from Purchases or Transfers of land):\nDeposits of these type of checks must be done separate from any other deposit including other Escrow Payments\nDo not combine multiple escrows checks in same deposit unless it is from a single buyer making a lump sum payment\nAttach all escrow documents to the deposit documents for the file\nTitle and Mortgage companies: (currently Weststar Mortgage)\nProcess same as Escrow Payments above\nInclude all ledger sheets with deposit documents\nEquipment Sales, Refunds, Rebates, Lawyer disbursements, etc\nProcess same as Escrow Payments above\nAttach all documents included with payment\nMaking a Deposit Packet – include the following in each packet\nPhotocopy all checks and deposit slips\nMultiple checks may be copied on same sheet\nMake sure all data on check is visible\nName and address\nCheck number\nAmount of check\nMicro encoding strip\nInclude the following in each separate packet\nPhotocopy of checks and deposit slip\nAny check stub provided by mortgage or escrow company\nAny real property sales paperwork or contracts\nAny equipment paperwork\nAny notes included with payment\nPaperclip each deposit as a packet separate from other deposits\nMake Deposit at Bank\nDeliver deposit slip and checks to bank for processing\nEnsure each Deposit Packet is a separate deposit\nReceive deposit receipt from bank\nInclude/attach a deposit receipt in each Deposit Packet\nPut packet together and hand to bank teller for pick up by TREASURER\nThis concludes the SECRETARY’s deposit responsibilities."}, {"id": "treasurer-checks", "title": "Check Payment Processing — Treasurer", "category": "Treasurer · Accounting Procedures", "description": "Procedures for receiving deposit packets, posting payments in the air-gapped QuickBooks system, reconciling deposits, printing summaries, and filing packets.", "pdf": "./documents/check-processing-treasurer.pdf", "odt": "./source/Check_Payment_Processing_Treasurer.odt", "content": "Check Payment Processing\nTREASURER\nReceive Deposit Packet from bank\nShould include deposit slip, bank receipt, copies of checks and any additional paperwork from Secretary.\nUsing the AirGapped Accounting Computer access QuickBooks link on desktop. DO NOT CONNECT THIS MACHINE TO THE INTERNET\nFor each check copy from each Deposit Packet:\nIn quickbooks, navigate to CUSTOMER\nTo locate Customer Record search records in this order under the Customers and Jobs tab\nBy last name\nBy parcel number\nBy city\nBy address\nAnalyze results to ascertain to which record to post payments and follow procedures below.\nIf unable to locate correct Customer Record:\nLocate customer named “unknown-holding-charity”\nPost Payment to “unknown-holding-charity” only when actual Customer Record cannot be ascertained from payment received.\nOnce correct Customer Record is located\nConfirm name and parcel number Customer List (left side)\nEnter EDIT mode to Confirm address, phone number, email address in customer details. Press OK.\nIf there is a different address, follow procedures under Address Procedures in this SOP.\nConfirm 911 address (if available)\nAdditions or changes to 911 addresses are recorded under the parcel (Job Information) details under “Additional Info” tab.\nRight click on Customer name and select Receive Payments from list at customer level (NOT job level).\nIn “Receive Payments” screen:\nSelect Payment Amount box and double click to select entire box, you can also press tab to reach this box.\nEnter full amount of payment received, press TAB to advance\nEnter the date shown on deposit slip ONLY, press TAB to advance\nSelect Check, Money Order, or Cashiers Check under payment type drop down\nUsing the Check number from Customer, enter it in Reference # box, omit any leading zeroes, press TAB to advance\nIn the MEMO box enter the YEAR and ASSESSMENT (ie… 2027 Assessment)\nIf multiple invoices are listed, select with a checkmark to which invoice payment should be applied (system defaults to oldest invoice. Change to invoice only applies if Customer requests funds be applied to different invoice)\nClick “Save and Close”\nIn red ink put checkmark in middle of photocopy of check\nRepeat this process for each check in Deposit Packet.\nIn quickbooks, process bank deposit\nUnder Banking Tab (top menu) Select Make Deposits\nIn “Payments to Deposit” window\nClick “Select All”, or if a single payment, enter checkmark for this payment. Deposit should ONLY include transactions pertaining to this deposit.\nConfirm quickbooks total is same as deposit slip and deposit receipt.\nClick “OK” to proceed\nThe “Make Deposits” page will be presented\nFrom Print menu, select Deposit Summary, press OK to open print window\nMake sure correct printer is selected and orientation is portrait\nClick “Print” button\nClick “Save and Close” button\nPrepare Deposit Packet for filing as follows in this order\nQuickbooks Deposit Summary on top\nBank deposit receipt, horizontally towards the top to allow for easy viewing\nPhotocopy of hand written deposit slip and the check photocopy sheets\nStaple together: one staple at the corner and one staple near the top middle\nFile in Monthly Income envelope.\nHolding Payment Processing\nContinue processing, adding details to a new note under “Notes” tab. Refer to Holding Payment Processing section for additional information."}, {"id": "directors", "title": "Current Directors and Officers", "category": "Board & Governance", "description": "The supplied leadership roster of River Springs Ranch POA directors, corporate officers, authorized signers, and reporting contacts.", "pdf": "./documents/directors-officers.pdf", "odt": "./source/Directors%20and%20Officers.odt", "content": "River Springs Ranch Property Owners Association\nCurrent Directors and Officers\nJanuary 19, 2026\n(Historically Validated Motions, As Per Recorded Board Meeting Minutes)\nKNOWN PLACE OF BUSINESS:\n262 CR N6410, St Johns, AZ 85936\n(911 address of current president)\nDIRECTORS: (all dates in this section pertain only to taking office as directors)\nSteward “Stu” C. Siebens\nPO Box 2248, St Johns, AZ 85936\nItem #6 of Mtg Minutes, 2/16/2017\nAuthorized Signer\nJohn Brooks\nPO Box 342, St Johns, AZ 85936\nItem #7a of Mtg Minutes, 11/29/2018\nAuthorized Signer\nDaniel Brooks\nPO Box 342, St Johns, AZ 85936\nItem #8 of Mtg Minutes, 4/18/2019\nNOT an Authorized Signer\nStephen Urban\nPO Box 424, St Johns, AZ 85936\nItem #3 of Mtg Minutes, 9/23/2023\nAuthorized Signer\nTami Riley\nPO Box 2842, St Johns, AZ 85936\nItem #4 of Mtg Minutes, 9/21/2024\nAuthorized Signer\nOFFICERS: (all dates in this section pertain only to taking office as officers)\nPresident\nSteward “Stu” C. Siebens\nItem #? of Mtg Minutes, 5/5/2024\nVice President\nStephen Urban\nItem #3 of Mtg Minutes, 1/18/2025\nSecretary\nTami Riley\nItem #7 of Mtg Minutes, 1/21/2022\nTreasurer\nJohn Brooks\nItem #7 of Mtg Minutes, 3/22/2018\nAll changes to Directors and Officers of the RSR POA must be reported to the Arizona Corporation Commission via Magnum, Wall, Stoops and Warden, PLLC (POA retained law firm and registered agent) at 100 N Eldon, PO Box 10, Flagstaff AZ 86002 and (928) 779-6951. See Changing and Reporting Directors and Officers section."}, {"id": "leadership-changes", "title": "Changing and Reporting Directors and Officers", "category": "Secretary · Board & Governance", "description": "Internal documentation and Arizona Corporation Commission reporting steps when directors or officers are elected, appointed, removed, or replaced.", "pdf": "./documents/changing-directors-officers.pdf", "odt": "./source/Changing%20and%20Reporting%20Directors%20and%20Officers.odt", "content": "Changing and Reporting Directors and Officers\nSECRETARY\nArizona Corporation Commission (ACC)\nDirect email request from the client.Changes to Directors and Officers of the Association must be reported to the Arizona Corporation Commission. Below is an explanation of what must take place and who is filing the changes with the ACC. This is for general understanding and knowledge.\nArizona Property Owners' Associations are incorporated as a nonprofit corporation and there are two separate actions whenever directors or officers change:\nThe POA makes the change internally according to its bylaws (election, appointment, resignation, removal, etc.).\nThe Arizona Corporation Commission (ACC) is notified so the public corporate record is updated.\nStep 1 – Document the change internally. The Board should:\nAccept any resignation (if applicable).\nElect or appoint the new director or officer in accordance with the bylaws.\nRecord the action in the official Board meeting minutes.\nUpdate the corporate records and officer/director roster under Directors and Officers.\nStep 2 – File with the Arizona Corporation Commission\nCurrently, the law firm Magnum, Wall, Stoops and Warden, PLLC (POA retained law firm and registered agent) at 100 N Eldon, PO Box 10, Flagstaff AZ 86002 and phone number (928) 779-6951, prepares and files any changes to Association leadership and also prepares and files the Association’s Annual Report.\nThere are two main instances for reporting a change of leadership to the ACC.\nDuring the Annual Meeting when new board members are either appointed or elected.\nAny time a Special1. Urgency of Action: A Special Meeting is appropriate when sufficient time exists to complete the required governance, administrative, legal, or operational actions before a deadline or other critical event (e.g., transferring bank authority following the election of new officers). or Emergency2. Urgency of Action: An Emergency Meeting is appropriate when immediate Board action is necessary because insufficient time exists to complete the required governance, administrative, legal, or operational actions before a deadline or other critical event. Meeting is called during the year for such an appointment.\n. Urgency of Action: A Special Meeting is appropriate when sufficient time exists to complete the required governance, administrative, legal, or operational actions before a deadline or other critical event (e.g., transferring bank authority following the election of new officers).\n. Urgency of Action: An Emergency Meeting is appropriate when immediate Board action is necessary because insufficient time exists to complete the required governance, administrative, legal, or operational actions before a deadline or other critical event.\nParagraphs 5, 6, and 7 are currently performed by the retained law firm and are for informational purposes only.\nIf the change occurs after the annual report has already been filed, the law firm submits an UPDATE by submitting an Officer/Director/Shareholder Change Form (Form C017). This form is specifically intended to update directors and officers during the year without waiting until the next annual report.\nIf your Annual Report is due soon, you may simply report the new directors and officers on the Annual Report instead of filing the separate change form.\nInformation you'll need:\nThe filing generally requires, on Association letterhead:\nExact legal name of the corporation.\nACC entity number.\nNames of directors/officers being removed.\nNames, addresses, and titles of newly elected/appointed directors/officers.\nSignature of an authorized corporate officer.\nWhere to file. You can:\nFile online through the ACC business portal (for eligible filings)\nMail or deliver the completed form to the Arizona Corporation Commission. The forms and instructions are available from the ACC's corporation forms page.\nPractical advice for the POA:\nWhen the POA elects/appoints directors/officers at the annual membership meeting, the following checklist is recommend to maintain continuity:\nMembership elects/appoints directors.\nBoard certifies election/appointment results.\nOrganizational meeting is held.\nOfficers (President, Vice President, Secretary, Treasurer) are elected by the Board.\nDirectors are elected by member acreage votes3. When not enough votes are returned to elect a director the election must then be canceled. Replacement directors can be appointed by the Board under these circumstances to continue POA operations.\n. When not enough votes are returned to elect a director the election must then be canceled. Replacement directors can be appointed by the Board under these circumstances to continue POA operations.\nMeeting minutes are approved.\nFile the ACC Officer/Director Change Form (or report the changes on the upcoming Annual Report, performed by informing the law firm on POA letterhead).\nUpdate the POA website, bank signature cards, insurance, and any vendor contact lists."}, {"id": "roles", "title": "Roles and Responsibilities", "category": "Board & Governance", "description": "The association’s working list of responsibilities for officers, directors, appointed positions, election staff, and hired road-maintenance staff.", "pdf": "./documents/roles-responsibilities.pdf", "odt": "./source/Roles%20Responsibilities.odt", "content": "Property Owners’ Association Roles and Responsibilities\nROLES AND RESPONSIBILITIES\nList of roles and responsibilities for Corporate Officers of the association.\nPresident:\nFinancial Authority:\nResponsible for all secure banking transactions, such as\nTransferring account authority to new president via official Association letterhead listing outgoing and incoming presidents\nAdding or removing authorized account signers\nCanceling and requesting new Visa debit card\nResponsibility Description.\nConduct Board Meetings:\nResponsible for conducting and directing pace of board meetings\nCan delegate this action to any of the Board members.\nVice President:\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nConduct Board Meetings:\nResponsible for conducting and directing pace of board meetings when delegated by the President\nSecretary:\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description.\nConduct Board Meetings:\nResponsible for conducting and directing pace of board meetings when delegated by the President\nTreasurer\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description.\nConduct Board Meetings:\nResponsible for conducting and directing pace of board meetings when delegated by the President\nDirectors\nMake Corporate decisions in the best interest of RSR POA:\nAttendance in River Springs Ranch (RSR) Board meetings and is familiar with Board discussions and governance.\nAvailability and willingness to get involved in ranch emergencies\nHave a good grasp of available local resources.\nHave involvements in local community affairs.\nDemonstrates a willingness to volunteer and contribute time and effort to River Springs Ranch projects and initiatives.\nNot be prone to anger issues or dishonesty.\nNot be prone to controlling others.\nParticipate in decisions requiring a vote:\nParticipation of the Directors in all aspects of the Organization.\nFollows through on commitments.\nMaintains a primary residence within the local area or is regularly present and actively engaged in the River Springs Ranch community.\nAbility to work within a group to reach to a consensus.\nMaintains an active, functioning account on the River Springs Ranch website (riverspringsranch.org) and is able to access community information and resources.\nDemonstrates Good Standing and Integrity\nHas a consistent history of paying annual assessments on time and remains in good standing with the Association.\nDemonstrates honesty, integrity, and ethical conduct, with no history of criminal activity involving dishonesty or conduct that would impair the ability to serve the Association.\nConduct Board Meetings:\nResponsible for conducting and directing pace of board meetings when delegated by the President\nList of roles and responsibilities for Appointed Positions of the association.\nRoad Chair\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description.\nElection Chair (temporary appointment during election year)\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description.\nElection Staff (temporary appointment during election year)\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description.\nList of roles and responsibilities for Hired Positions of the association.\nRoad Maintenance Staff\nMain Role 1:\nResponsibility Description..\nResponsibility Description..\nMain Role 2:\nResponsibility Description.\nResponsibility Description."}, {"id": "surety", "title": "CNA Surety Bond", "category": "Secretary · Insurance & Protection", "description": "An overview of the POA’s fidelity and crime bond, examples of covered loss, items generally not covered, and annual administration responsibilities.", "pdf": "./documents/surety-bond.pdf", "odt": "./source/Surety%20Bond.odt", "content": "Surety Bond\nSECRETARY\nCNA Surety\nCNA Surety provides a full range of surety and fidelity bonds in all 50 states, Canada and Puerto Rico. Through a combined network of approximately 40,000 appointed agencies, CNA Surety has the capacity, experience and dedication to serve a full range of bonding needs, from the smallest commercial bonds to multi-million dollar contract bonds.\nPoint of Contact\nLincoln Towne Center\n4150 N. Drinkwater Blvd., Suite 410\nScottsdale, AZ 85251\nPh: 866-521-3309 Fax: 480-941-3251 Website: https://www.cnasurety.com/\nRiver Springs Ranch Property Owners' Association (POA) is using CNA Surety for its fidelity/crime bonding. This is not for general liability insurance.\nHere's what they provide to a POA:\nFidelity Bond (Employee/Volunteer Dishonesty)\nThis is the most common reason a POA has CNA Surety. It protects the association if someone handling the POA's money steals or misuses it, including:\nBoard members\nTreasurer\nOfficers\nVolunteers\nManagement company employees (depending on the policy)\nBookkeeper\nFor example:\nTreasurer writes themselves a $20,000 check.\nManagement company employee diverts dues into a personal account.\nSomeone forges checks from the POA account.\nThe bond reimburses the association for covered losses.\nHOA/POA Loan Requirements\nMany banks require an association to carry a fidelity bond before they'll:\nOpen certain accounts\nExtend loans\nRefinance association debt\nProtection of Reserve Funds\nMany states and governing documents recommend—or require—that reserve accounts be bonded.\nIf your POA has:\n$500,000 in reserves\n$150,000 operating account\nThe bond amount is often written to cover all or most of those funds.\nProtection Against Fraud\nSome CNA Surety policies can also include coverage for:\nCheck forgery\nComputer fraud\nWire transfer fraud\nEmployee theft\nCredit card fraud\nThe exact coverages depend on the policy purchased.\nWhat CNA Surety does not cover\nA fidelity bond generally does not pay for:\nRoof damage\nStorm damage\nSlip-and-fall injuries\nLawsuits against the association\nDirectors making poor business decisions (that's typically covered by Directors & Officers insurance)\nGeneral liability claims\nThose are covered by separate insurance policies.\nWhy the POA has historically secured the services of a surety company\nIf the association collects:\nAnnual dues\nSpecial assessments\nReserve funds\nInvestment accounts\nThe board has a fiduciary duty to protect those assets. A fidelity bond is one of the primary ways to do that.\nIf you are on the board\nIt's worth confirming:\nThe bond limit (is it high enough to cover funds under your control?)\nWho is covered (board members, volunteers, management company, etc.)\nWhether computer and wire fraud are included\nWhat other things are covered in the fidelity bond\nIs there an online account? Where do we create one?\nWhat is the POA’s surety account number?\nRequest new documents that provide all details, coverages and claim information, including a declaration page.\nThe SECRETARY is responsible to make the yearly payments following the procedures for Making Payments."}];
